// ═══ TIGHT COUPLING: TradeService hardwired to SmsService ═══
public class TradeService {
    private final SmsService smsService = new SmsService();  // HARDWIRED!
    private final PostgresTradeRepository tradeRepo = new PostgresTradeRepository();  // HARDWIRED!

    public void executeTrade(Trade trade) {
        tradeRepo.save(trade);                          // LOCKED to PostgreSQL
        smsService.send(trade.getAccountId(), "Trade executed");  // LOCKED to SMS
    }
}
// PROBLEMS:
// 1. Can't switch to EmailService without modifying TradeService
// 2. Can't test without real PostgreSQL database
// 3. Can't test without sending real SMS
// 4. Any change to SmsService/PostgresTradeRepository may break TradeService
// 5. TradeService has TWO responsibilities: business logic + choosing dependencies
```

```java
// ═══ LOOSE COUPLING: TradeService depends on INTERFACES ═══
public class TradeService {
    private final NotificationService notifier;      // INTERFACE — not concrete!
    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;          // INTERFACE — not concrete!

    // Dependencies INJECTED from outside (DI):
    public TradeService(NotificationService notifier, TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.notifier = notifier;
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    public void executeTrade(Trade trade) {
        tradeRepo.save(trade);                        // could be Postgres, MongoDB, InMemory
        notifier.send(trade.getAccountId(), "Trade executed");  // could be SMS, Email, Mock
    }
}
// BENEFITS:
// 1. Swap SMS → Email: inject EmailService. ZERO changes to TradeService.
// 2. Test: inject MockNotificationService + InMemoryTradeRepository. No real DB or SMS!
// 3. SmsService changes internally → TradeService unaffected (depends on interface).
// 4. TradeService has ONE responsibility: business logic. DI container handles wiring.
```

Four signs of TIGHT coupling and their fixes:

```java
// SIGN 1: 'new' inside the class (creating dependencies directly)
// (Avoid) private final SmsService sms = new SmsService();
// (Recommended) private final NotificationService notifier;  // injected!

// SIGN 2: concrete class in method signature
// (Avoid) public void process(PostgresTradeRepository repo) { }
// (Recommended) public void process(TradeRepository repo) { }  // interface!

// SIGN 3: static method calls to concrete utilities
// (Avoid) String hash = CryptoUtils.sha256(data);
// (Recommended) String hash = hashService.hash(data);  // inject HashService interface

// SIGN 4: direct field access on another class
// (Avoid) account.balance = amount;  // accessing internal state directly
// (Recommended) account.deposit(amount);   // using public method (encapsulation!)
```

WHY loose coupling matters — three production benefits:

```java
// BENEFIT 1: SWAPPABLE at runtime (Strategy pattern)
// Production: new TradeService(new SmsService(), new PostgresRepo())
// A/B test:   new TradeService(new EmailService(), new PostgresRepo())
// Migration:  new TradeService(new SmsService(), new MongoRepo())
// ZERO code changes in TradeService!

// BENEFIT 2: TESTABLE with mocks
@Test void executeTrade_sendsNotification() {
    NotificationService mock = mock(NotificationService.class);
    TradeRepository mockRepo = mock(TradeRepository.class);
    TradeService service = new TradeService(mock, mockRepo);

    service.executeTrade(new Trade("TRD-001", "RELIANCE", amount));

    verify(mock).send("SAV2001", "Trade executed");  // verified without real SMS!
    // Test runs in 10ms. No database. No SMS gateway.
}

// BENEFIT 3: MAINTAINABLE — change implementation without breaking clients
// SmsService switches from Twilio to AWS SNS → zero changes to TradeService.
// TradeService depends on NotificationService interface — implementation details hidden.
```

